In this code, `Process.GetProcessesByName("foo")` is used to get all the processes with the name 'foo'. 

If the length of the array returned by `Process.GetProcessesByName("foo")` is 0, it means no 'foo' processes are currently running so we start a new one.

If a 'foo' process is already running, it is brought to the foreground using `ShowWindow` and `SetForegroundWindow`. These methods are in the `user32.dll` library so we use `DllImport` to import them. The `ShowWindow` method is used to show a window that was previously hidden (the process's window in this case). The `SetForegroundWindow` method is used to bring a window (again, the process's window in this case) to the foreground (i.e., makes it the active window). 

The common names for processes are usually the executable file name without the `.exe` part. So if your executable file is named `foo.exe`, the process name will be 'foo'. Be noted that the process name is case-sensitive.

Also remember that it may not always be possible to bring a window to the foreground programmatically. The system has certain restrictions, you can find out more about them in the documentation of the `SetForegroundWindow` function: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-setforegroundwindow.

