Here is a Java solution that uses a Map to keep a count of each character. It then constructs a List of count-character pairs, sorts this List by count in reverse order and then converts the List to a string that resembles Python's list of tuples syntax for the output.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(getCharacterFrequency("Hello World"));
        System.out.println(getCharacterFrequency("An unusual sentence with unusual characters like #%$^%*"));
        System.out.println(getCharacterFrequency(""));
        System.out.println(getCharacterFrequency(null));
    }

    public static String getCharacterFrequency(String str) {
        // Handle null string
        if (str == null) {
            return "[]";
        }

        // Create a Map to keep count of characters
        Map<Character, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : str.toLowerCase().toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
                counts.put(c, counts.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
            }
        }

        // Create a List of count-character pairs
        List<CountCharPair> countCharPairs = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
            countCharPairs.add(new CountCharPair(entry.getValue(), entry.getKey()));
        }

        // Sort by count in reverse order
        countCharPairs.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder());

        // Convert the List to a string similar to Python's list of tuples
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("[");
        for (int i = 0; i < countCharPairs.size(); i++) {
            if (i > 0) {
                sb.append(", ");
            }
            sb.append(countCharPairs.get(i));
        }
        sb.append("]");

        return sb.toString();
    }

    static class CountCharPair implements Comparable<CountCharPair> {
        int count;
        char character;

        CountCharPair(int count, char character) {
            this.count = count;
            this.character = character;
        }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(CountCharPair o) {
            return count != o.count ? Integer.compare(count, o.count) : Character.compare(character, o.character);
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return String.format("('%c', %d)", character, count);
        }
    }

}
```

