```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

class Point {
    int i, j, sum;
    Point(int i, int j, int sum) {
        this.i = i;
        this.j = j;
        this.sum = sum;
    }
}

public List<Integer> minSumPath(int[][] grid, int k) {
    int n = grid.length;
    int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
    for(int[] row : dp)
        Arrays.fill(row, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
    dp[0][0] = grid[0][0];

    PriorityQueue<Point> queue = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a.sum - b.sum);
    queue.offer(new Point(0, 0, dp[0][0]));

    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
    int[][] directions = new int[][]{{0, 1}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {-1, 0}};

    while(!queue.isEmpty() && result.size() < k) {
        Point cur = queue.poll();
        result.add(cur.sum);

        for(int[] dir : directions) {
            int ni = cur.i + dir[0];
            int nj = cur.j + dir[1];

            if(ni >= 0 && ni < n && nj >= 0 && nj < n) {
                int newSum = cur.sum + grid[ni][nj];
                if(newSum < dp[ni][nj]) {
                    dp[ni][nj] = newSum;
                    queue.offer(new Point(ni, nj, newSum));
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

These changes were made to convert the code:
1. Python modules were replaced with Java classes and methods.
2. Can't use tuples (immutable collections) in Java, so I created a simple class `Point` to hold data.
3. Lambda functions (anonymous functions) were used to create a comparator for the priority queue.
4. Python list methods replaced with ArrayList methods in Java.
5. Python loop 'for dir in directions' replaced with extended for loop in Java.
6. Indices for multi-dimension arrays in Java follow a different syntax `[i][j]` instead of `[i, j]` in Python.
7. ArrayList used instead of python list in Java.
8. Methods called on objects and arrays follow a different syntax.

