    fn1 = () => {}
    fn1 = () => {}
    constructor() {
        super(
            this.fn1,
            this.fn2
        )
    }
}



But I get the error "must call super constructor before accessing 'this'".
I know why this is happening, and I have an idea of how to fix it (wrapping `this.fn1` like `() => this.fn1()`), but... I like conciseness.
Is there a way I can get away with passing references to a childs methods into a super call?

This problem arises because when calling super, you cannot access any instance properties or methods (anything that uses this keyword) beforehand, as they are not yet initialized. So when you are passing this.fn1 and this.fn2 to super, JavaScript interpreter doesn’t know what this is.

A possible solution is defining the methods on the prototype, rather than as instance properties:

```javascript
class Base {
    constructor(...fns) {
        //Store fns for later call
    }
}

class Derived extends Base {
    constructor() {
        super(
            Derived.prototype.fn1.bind(this),
            Derived.prototype.fn2.bind(this)
        )
    }
}

Derived.prototype.fn1 = function() {
    // some logic...
}

Derived.prototype.fn2 = function() {
    // some logic...
}
```

Note that the .bind(this) is necessary, because the method will otherwise not be bound to the correct this context. Alternatively, you could use arrow functions, which are automatically bound to the this context at the time they are declared. Here it can't be used directly in super call as arrow functions use local instance this.

In some ways this is a bit more cumbersome than using instance properties directly, but it's a viable workaround to your problem.

