Propose an optimization for the C++ for-loop below, specifically targeting its efficiency, while considering the array processing and target value comparison.
for(int i = 0; i<arr.length; i++){
    if(arr[i] == target){
        return true;
    }
}

The rationale behind any optimization would be either reducing the number of iterations or improving the comparison efficiency. However, checking each element is necessary in this case because the target could be located anywhere in the array. The comparison itself is as efficient as it can be.

One possible optimization if the input array arr is sorted, we can use a binary search algorithm which has a time complexity of O(log n) compared to the linear O(n). If the array is not sorted we can either sort and use binary search which has a time complexity of O(n log n), or store values in a `std::unordered_set` for O(1) lookup.

However, if we don't know anything about how the target is distributed, the given loop already achieves best possible efficiency of O(n). 

Here is a proposal to do this using the unordered set in C++:

```cpp
#include<unordered_set>

bool checkTarget(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    std::unordered_set<int> hashSet;
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if(hashSet.find(target) != hashSet.end()) {
            return true;
        }
        hashSet.insert(arr[i]);
    }
    return false;
}
```

Be sure to note the trade-offs though. While we achieve faster lookups with an unordered set, we do increase our memory usage as we're storing elements in the set. As a general rule, it's always important to understand these trade-offs when optimizing any code. In some cases, memory usage might be more of a concern than time complexity. In others, it's the opposite. It's all about choosing the right tool for the right job.

