How can I fix this?

In BDDMockito when you want to mock a method invocation you use BDDMockito#willAnswer and BDDMockito#given. But when you want to stub a method, you should use Mockito#doAnswer.

When you are saying given(spy.put(any())), it's actually calling the method put. Instead, you should just pass "spy::put" as a method reference.

Additionally, for a method inside an interface, you may need to write a Spy that mock the behavior for the interface method, not use the BDDMockito.

Here's how you could achieve the behavior you want:

```java
MyInterface myInterface = mock(MyInterface.class);
```

```java
doAnswer(i -> {
    ByteBuffer buffer = i.getArgument(0);
    assert buffer != null;
    print(buffer);
    assert buffer.capacity() == BYTES;
    assert buffer.limit() == buffer.capacity();
    assert buffer.remaining() == BYTES;
    buffer.position(buffer.limit());
    return buffer;
}).when(myInterface).put(any());
```

Now you can use `myInterface` in tests and it should be working just as you expected. Mocking is creating objects that simulate the behavior of real objects. A Spy, on the other hand, is a partial mock, wrapping around the real object. Hence, you should use mock in this case.

