Yes, you can unit test a COM-visible .NET assembly by using the COM interop to interact with the assembly instead of a direct .NET assembly reference. Here are the steps you would need to follow:

1. Build and register the assembly as COM-visible: On the properties window in Visual Studio, under the build tab, check "Register for COM interop". Also make sure "Make assembly COM-Visible" option is enabled in Assembly Information dialog window.

2. Generate a COM Interop assembly/items: You can auto-generate an interop assembly/items using tools like `tlbimp.exe` (which comes with .NET SDK) or by adding a reference to the COM component to a .NET project (Visual studio generates the Interop assembly when you add the reference).

3. In the test project, add reference to this Interop assembly, not the original assembly.

4. Write your unit tests in .NET to interact with the COM-visible assembly via the Interop assembly. 

For example, if your COM visible .NET assembly had a class and method like this:

```
[ComVisible(true)]
public class Calculation
{
    public int Sum(int num1, int num2)
    {
        return num1 + num2;
    }
}
```

Your unit test would look something like this:

```
public class CalculationTest
{
    // Reference to your COM-visible object via the Interop assembly
    private dynamic calc;

    [SetUp]
    public void Setup()
    {
        // Instantiate your COM-visible object via the Interop assembly
        calc = Activator.CreateInstance(Type.GetTypeFromProgID("YourProgId.Calculation"));
    }

    [Test]
    public void TestSum()
    {
        int result = calc.Sum(2, 3);
        Assert.AreEqual(5, result);
    }
}
```

Remember to replace `"YourProgId.Calculation"` with the correct ProgID of your COM component.

Please note, Com Interop adds overhead, and testing via Com Interop is slower than testing directly via .NET references. It's recommended to use this method only for necessary scenarios, like verifying compatibility with COM clients.

