Here's a simple program that demonstrates the use of the `volatile` keyword. Please note that unpredictable behavior driven by missing `volatile` is heavily dependent on factors such as the JVM, hardware, caching strategy etc., so it's not guaranteed that this will manifest the issue in all cases.

```java
public class VolatileExample implements Runnable {
    private static/*volatile*/ int counter = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread thread1 = new Thread(new VolatileExample(), "Thread 1");
        Thread thread2 = new Thread(new VolatileExample(), "Thread 2");
        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();
        thread1.join();
        thread2.join();
        System.out.println("The final value of counter is " + counter);
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for(int i=0;i<1000000;i++) {
            incrementCounter();
        }
    }

    private synchronized void incrementCounter() {
        counter++;
    }
}
```
In this example, two threads increment a shared counter. If you run this code enough times or in a specific JVM setup, you may find that the final counter value is not 2000000 (which is expected if both threads increment the counter a million times each). This can happen because the update of the `counter` by one thread might not stay visible to the other thread if `counter` was cached by the CPU for the thread.

You can remove the comments around the `volatile` keyword to make the `counter` variable volatile. The `volatile` keyword in Java is used to mark a Java variable as "being stored in main memory". More precisely that means, that every read of a volatile variable will be read from the computer's main memory, and not from the CPU cache, and that every write to a volatile variable will be written to main memory, and not just to the CPU cache.

So, making `counter` volatile should fix the problem, and the final counter value should be consistently 2000000 because a volatile variable will make sure the value of `counter` is properly synchronized between threads. In other words, all actions performed on the volatile variable are atomic.

However, as I noted earlier, not all systems and JVMs will demonstrate the issue with the counter being less than 2000000 because modern JVMs are pretty intelligent in handling such situations. So, `volatile` becomes crucial when writing robust multithreaded code but demonstrating its impact can be tricky.

In production systems, more complex and realistic scenarios requiring use of `volatile` are around the cases where we perform check-then-act e.g. double-checked locking idiom.

