Polymorphism in Java is identified as one of the OOPs feature that allows us to perform a single action in numerous ways. Polymorphism is derived from two Greek words: poly (means many) and morphs (means forms). Therefore, polymorphism in Java is the capability that permits an object to take on many forms. The most common use of polymorphism in Java Occurs when a parent class reference is used to point towards a child class object.

There are two types of polymorphism in Java: compile-time polymorphism (static binding or method overloading) and runtime polymorphism (dynamic binding or method overriding).

Compile-time polymorphism is method overloading whereas Runtime time polymorphism is done using inheritance and interface. When in a class we have more than one methods with the same name but different properties, we call it as method overloading. On the other hand, when a method in a class having the same method as a parent class, it is called as method overriding.

For example:

```Java

//Java Program to show example of polymorphism

//Parent Class
class Bird { 
    void fly() { 
        System.out.println("The bird is flying"); 
    } 
} 

//Child Class
class Sparrow extends Bird { 
    //method is overridden here
    void fly() { 
        System.out.println("The sparrow is flying"); 
    } 
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) { 
        //creating objects 
        Bird bird1 = new Bird(); 
        Bird bird2 = new Sparrow(); 

        //calling the method
        bird1.fly(); //output : The bird is flying
        bird2.fly(); //output : The sparrow is flying
    } 
}
```
In the above example, we can see that the parent class Bird method fly() is called by the object bird1. Whereas, the child class Sparrow fly() method is being called by the object bird2. So, even though we have same method fly(), different results are being produced. This is an example of runtime polymorphism.

On execution, the JVM determines the proper methods to call at the program’s run time, not at the compile time. This process is known as dynamic method dispatch and demonstrates an aspect of what is often referred to as late binding or runtime polymorphism.

