    });
```

When the web page is loaded, a request is made to your API URL. The response from this request is a Promise which when it resolves, we check if the HTTP status is ok (200). If it's not, we throw an error and catch it later in the catch block.

If the HTTP response is ok, we continue processing the Promise which contains the body of the response. In this case, we expect it to be a JSON structure so we parse it with the `response.json()` method which itself returns a Promise resolving with the contents of the body parsed as JSON.

Once this Promise resolves successfully, meaning the body was successfully parsed as JSON, we then have access to the JSON data in the next `then()` clause. In this case, the data is an object with name, age, and address properties and we can simply access these properties like any properties of an object in JavaScript with the dot '.' operator.

We then get the DOM elements with IDs 'name', 'age', and 'address' and set their textContent property to the respective values in the received JSON object. 

In conclusion, while HTML cannot fetch or display JSON data by itself, incorporating JavaScript allows us to retrieve and manipulate JSON data as shown above. Once you have fetched and parsed the JSON data, you can leverage the DOM (Document Object Model) to integrate this data into your HTML elements.

