Here is a C++ implementation using template, you'll need to define your own Node structure including your complex data.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
#include<string>
using namespace std; 

template <class T>
struct Node {
    T value;
    Node<T> *next = nullptr, *prev = nullptr;
};

template <class T>
void deleteNode(Node<T>* &head, T value) {
    if (!head) return;

    Node<T> *temp = head;
    while (temp) {
        if (temp->value == value) {
            // If node to be deleted is head node 
            if (head == temp)
                head = temp->next;

            // Change next and prev only if they are not NULL
            if (temp->next != NULL)
                temp->next->prev = temp->prev;
            if (temp->prev != NULL)
                temp->prev->next = temp->next;

            // Finally, free the memory occupied by temp
            delete temp;

            // Set temp to the next node (if it exists)
            temp = head;
        } else {
            temp = temp->next;
        }
    }
}
```

In the `deleteNode` function, we ensure to properly change the 'next' and 'previous' pointers of the neighboring nodes before the 'deletion' (memory freeing) occurs so that no links are broken in the process. Also, to handle duplicates, if a match is found, we do not increment `temp`'s position, because we've changed `temp` to its `next` node when we delete it.

Please note that efficient management of memory resources in C++ can be tricky and it's important to take care that any 'deleted' nodes are no longer accessible to prevent potential "dangling pointer" situations. Additionally, deleting elements does not automatically adjust the size of the container, so this must be managed manually. Lastly, this code does not handle complex data comparison directly, you need to overload the `==` operator.

