Given the following erroneous CSS code, identify and correct the errors for the purpose of positioning a <div> element centrally aligned in both vertical and horizontal directions in the viewport without affecting the responsiveness of the layout. Additionally, explain in detail why these changes are required.

```css
div {
  position: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

The CSS code given has some incorrect properties. Here is the corrected code:

```css
div {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

Here is the detailed explanation of the changes:

1. `position: center;` - There's no `center` value for the `position` property in CSS. The correct way to center a `div` both vertically and horizontally is by using `position: absolute;`, alongside `top: 50%;` and `left: 50%;`, to shift the `div`'s top left corner to the center of its parent/viewport. But this will not center the `div` exactly, it will just place the top left corner at the center. To actually center our `div`, we need to offset it half of its own width to the left and half of its height upwards. We can do that easily using `transform: translate(-50%, -50%);`.

2. `align-items: center;` - This property is used inside a flex container to center its children along the cross axis. For it to work, the `div` need to be a flex container, so `display: flex;` is necessary. To center along the main axis as well, we also add `justify-content: center;`.

These adjustments will enable the `div` to be centrally positioned both vertically and horizontally, and not disrupt the responsiveness of the layout as the percentages will adjust based on the viewport size. Keep in mind, this positions the `div` according to the nearest positioned ancestor (instead of relative to the viewport). If you want to position the `div` relative to the viewport, you can use `position: fixed;` instead of `position: absolute;`.

This solution assume the width and height of the div is fixed or content defined, for full responsiveness you will need to make sure you're using flexible widths/heights and make other responsive design considerations.

