The code you've posted above shows you redirecting your user to the GitHub authorization page, but for a full OAuth flow you need more steps.

The basic flow would be:

1. After your user authorizes your application on GitHub, GitHub will redirect back to your application with a `code` as part of the URL query string.

2. Your backend server then needs to exchange this code for an access token via the `https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token` according to the GitHub OAuth documentation.

Here is an example in NodeJs express:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/github/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const code = req.query.code;

  if (!code) {
    return res.send({
      success: false,
      message: 'Error: no code'
    });
  }

  const response = await axios({
    method: 'post',
    url: `https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token?client_id=${clientID}&client_secret=${clientSecret}&code=${code}`,
    headers: {
     accept: 'application/json'
    }
  });

  const accessToken = response.data.access_token;
  const refreshToken = response.data.refresh_token;

  res.send({accessToken, refreshToken});
});

``` 

In this example, you make a POST request to the GitHub `access_token` endpoint with your client id, client secret and the code received. The response will contain your access token and refresh token. 

Remember to replace `clientID` and `clientSecret` with your actual GitHub OAuth application's client id and secret.

You need to set `https://your-server.com/github/callback` (replace `https://your-server.com` with your actual server's domain) as your OAuth app's "Authorization callback URL" on your Github OAuth application settings page. This is the route GitHub will redirect to after the user authorizes your app.

Be sure to secure your access token and refresh token on your server side as they can be used to make requests on behalf of the user.

