To solve this problem, we can use a recursive approach to search the binary search tree. The method will take the root of the tree and the key to search for as arguments, and it will return a boolean indicating if the key is present in the tree. 

If the tree is empty (the root is null), then our base case would return false. 

Otherwise, if the key equals the value of the root, the function will return true. 

If the key is less than the root's value, the function will call itself with the root's left child as the new root. 

If the key is greater than the root's value, the function will call itself with the root's right child.

The algorithm has a complexity of O(log N) when the tree is balanced and O(N) in the worst case when it is not balanced because you may have to traverse through all the nodes. 

Here is the java solution:

```java
// Node definition for Binary Search Tree(BST)
class Node {
    int key;
    Node left, right;

    public Node(int item) {
        key = item;
        left = right = null;
    }
}

// A Java program to introduce Binary Tree
class BinaryTree
{
    // Root of Binary Tree
    Node root;

    /* Function to check if a key is present in the tree */
    boolean isPresent(Node root, int key) {
        /* base cases */
        if( root == null) {
            return false;
        }
        if (root.key == key) {
            return true;
        }

        /* If key is greater than root's key then key must be present in right subtree */
        if(root.key < key){
            return isPresent(root.right, key);
        }

        /* If key is less than root's key then key must be present in left subtree */
        else {
            return isPresent(root.left, key);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        BinaryTree tree = new BinaryTree();

        /*create root*/
        tree.root = new Node(20);

        /* following is the tree after above statement

              20
             /  \
           null  null     */

        tree.root.left = new Node(10);
        tree.root.right = new Node(30);
        tree.root.left.left = new Node(5);
        tree.root.left.right = new Node(15);

        /* the constructed binary tree is
                20
               /  \
             10    30
            /  \
           5   15
        */

        System.out.println("Key 10 present in BST : " + tree.isPresent(tree.root, 10));
        System.out.println("Key 40 present in BST : " + tree.isPresent(tree.root, 40));

    }
}
```

This Binary tree implementation first creates a simple binary tree and then it uses the isPresent() helper method to check if various keys are present in the tree.

