How many bytes of memory can a 32-bit system address?

Analyze the following erroneous code to determine the maximum capacity of a 32-bit system:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  long maxMemory = 2 ^ 32;
  printf("Max Memory (bytes): %ld\n", maxMemory);
  return 0;
}
```

Additionally, explain the error in the code and provide the correct code snippet to calculate the maximum memory capacity.

A 32-bit system can address 2^32 bytes of memory. However, in the given code, the caret (^) symbol is not correctly used. In C, the (^) symbol is used for bitwise XOR operation, not for exponentiation. Therefore, in this case the "2 ^ 32" operation will not provide the expected result (4294967296), but instead will do a XOR operation between 2 and 32, which equals 34.

The correct code using bit left shift operation (<<) to calculate 2^32:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  unsigned long maxMemory = 1ul << 32;
  printf("Max Memory (bytes): %lu\n", maxMemory);
  return 0;
}
```

Note: "1ul << 32" means shift the bits of the number "1" to the left 32 places, effectively multiplying "1" by 2^32. Also, an unsigned long is used because the result might be larger than what a signed long can represent.

Also note that you can run this program on a 64-bit system because 32-bit system can't handle variable of size more than 4GB (2^32 bytes) due to the addressing limitation.

