Identify and correct the error, ensuring the function works as expected.

The initial code doesn't work correctly because it does not initialise the variable max. It uses it to store the number if it's even but there can be a case where we might not find an even number then in that case we won't be initialising the max variable and return statement at end would give an error because it uses max variable which is not initialised yet. Moreover, if for example range [1,2] is given as input, the even number would be 2. However, since our loop starts from x (i.e., 1) and checks that 1 is not even, it would then move to next value i.e., 2 which is our last value and it would set max as 2 and returns 2 as maximum even number which is correct but the issue is we are not checking for next value in next iteration because our loop is running till <= y but it won't go for y+1 value as it would be out of range provided, so our loop should only run till < y. Hence we are modifying our code to initialise our max variable at start itself and changing loop running till y instead of <= y.

Corrected Code:

```cpp
#include <stdio.h>

using namespace std;

int choose_num(int x,int y){

    // first check if the y is odd or even
    // if odd decrement by 1 to make it even
    // because we are checking the greatest 
    // even number

    if(y % 2 != 0){
        y--;
    }

    // now check if modified y is less than x then
    // it means there is no even number between the range
    // hence return -1

    if(y < x){
        return -1;
    }

    // if the above two conditions passed then return
    // the greatest even number
    return y;
}
```
This approach cuts down unnecessary for loop iterations thus making it more efficient in terms of time complexity. It directly calculates the greatest even number between the given range.

