Here is a solution using JavaScript:

```javascript
function checkEven(array) {
    // Check if array is empty
    if(array.length === 0) return true;

    let sum = 0;
    let product = 1;
    const isEven = num => num % 2 === 0;

    for(let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
        if(!isEven(array[i])) {
            return false;
        }
        sum += array[i];
        product *= array[i];
   }

   return isEven(sum) && isEven(product);
}
```
This function works by first initializing the sum to 0 and product to 1. It then loops through each number in the array and checks if it is an even number. If it encounters an odd number, it immediately returns false. Otherwise, it adds the number to the sum and includes it in the product. After checking all the numbers, it returns true only if both the sum and product are even. The function also treats an empty array as meeting the conditions (since all zero elements are even, and the sum and product of no numbers are both considered to be zero, which is even).

