Implement a two-stack queue data structure to store and retrieve items in last-in first-out and first-in first-out order. The queue should consist of two stacks - one for enqueuing and the other for dequeuing items.

For reference, consider a buggy implementation of a single stack:

```
class Stack {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
  }

  push(item) {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  pop() {
    return this.items.pop();
  }

  peek() {
    return this.items[this.items.length];
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.items.length === 0;
  }
}
``` 
The "peek()" function in this reference code incorrectly tries to access an element at the array's length index (which doesn't exist, as JavaScript array indices start from 0), instead of the last element before that. It needs to be fixed.

Here is the corrected implementation of the Stack class:

```javascript
class Stack {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
  }

  push(item) {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  pop() {
    return this.items.pop();
  }

  peek() {
    return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.items.length === 0;
  }
}
```

Now we create a Queue class using the corrected Stack class:

```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.enqueueStack = new Stack();
    this.dequeueStack = new Stack();
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    this.enqueueStack.push(item);
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.dequeueStack.isEmpty()) {
      while (!this.enqueueStack.isEmpty()) {
        this.dequeueStack.push(this.enqueueStack.pop());
      }
    }
    return this.dequeueStack.pop();
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.enqueueStack.isEmpty() && this.dequeueStack.isEmpty();
  }
}
```
The idea here is to push new elements onto the enqueue stack. When we need to dequeue an element, we check if the dequeue stack is empty. If it is, we pop all elements from the enqueue stack and push them onto the dequeue stack. In this way, the last element added to the enqueue stack is now the first one to be popped from the dequeue stack, preserving the FIFO characteristic of the queue. If the dequeue stack is not empty, we simply pop an element from it.

