In addition to just finding the solution, also take into account potential performance issues.

The above code doesn't work because `getElementsByTagName` returns a `NodeList`, which we need to iterate over to apply styles. 

Moreover, we need to remember the original color for each element so that we can revert it back. Here's a more robust solution using JavaScript:

```javascript
var originalColors = {};

function toGreen() {
    for (var i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
        let tagName = 'h' + i;
        let elements = document.getElementsByTagName(tagName);

        for (var j = 0; j < elements.length; j++) {
            let element = elements[j];

            if (!originalColors[element]) {
                originalColors[element] = element.style.color;
            }

            element.style.color = 'green';
        }
    }
}

function revertColors() {
    for (var i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
        let tagName = 'h' + i;
        let elements = document.getElementsByTagName(tagName);

        for (var j = 0; j < elements.length; j++) {
            let element = elements[j];

            if (originalColors[element]) {
                element.style.color = originalColors[element];
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this solution, we iterate over all heading tags from `h1` to `h6` and within each tag we loop through all elements. We store the current element color before changing it. When `revertColors()` is called, we restore the original colors from our saved object.

