These types of statements are known as conditional comments and they are specifically designed to target versions of Internet Explorer. The comment you posted:

```html
<!--[if IE]>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="ie.css" type="text/css" />
<![endif]-->
```
With this, the enclosed link (to an alternative IE-specific style sheet in your example) will only been seen and used by Internet Explorer. Other browsers ignore the comment because they interpret it as a standard HTML comment and do not action its content.

This mechanism is designed to help manage the historically problematic rendering issues associated with Internet Explorer. So, this doesn't work across all modern browsers as you asked - it's an IE-specific feature.

This feature is essentially obsolete now given that post IE 10, Microsoft removed support for conditional comments, and the latest versions of internet explorer and Microsoft Edge are far more consistent with other browsers in terms of their CSS parsing and rendering.

If you want to use different style sheets for different browsers, a better approach these days is to use feature detection rather than browser detection, which modern javascript libraries like Modernizr help you do. This is more robust and future-proofed way to manage browser inconsistencies. Example:

```javascript
if (Modernizr.canvas) {
  // browser supports canvas; do something...
} else {
  // no canvas support; do something else...
}
```
