Let's define a simple node class for our linked list in Java:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int d) {
        data = d;
        next = null;
    }
}
```

Now we can define our algorithm to reverse the list. We will use three pointers: `previous`, `current` and `next`. 

At each step, we first save a reference to the `current` node's `next` node in `next`. Then we reverse the `current` node's pointer to point to `previous` instead. Finally, we move our `previous` and `current` pointers one step forward so `previous` points to `current` node and `current` points to `next` node. This will be done in a loop until `current` is `null`.

```java
class Solution {
    static Node head;  // head of list

    /* Function to reverse the linked list */
    static Node reverse(Node node) {
        Node previous = null;
        Node current = node;
        Node next = null;
        while (current != null) {
            next = current.next;
            current.next = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = next;
        }
        node = previous;
        return node;
    }

    /* Function to print linked list */
    static void printList(Node node) {
        while (node != null) {
            System.out.print(node.data + " ");
            node = node.next;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution list = new Solution();
        list.head = new Node(1);
        list.head.next = new Node(2);
        list.head.next.next = new Node(3);
        list.head.next.next.next = new Node(4);
        list.head.next.next.next.next = new Node(5);

        System.out.println("Given Linked list");
        list.printList(head);
        head = list.reverse(head);
        System.out.println("");
        System.out.println("Reversed linked list ");
        list.printList(head);
    }
}
```

