Although your solution may seem logically correct, there is a problem that may occur when currentNode = currentNode.next. In this case if the value of currentNode.next is equal to the given value (val), an infinite cycle problem will arise. 

We can overcome this problem by checking the if currentNode is null at the start of the function and loop until the currentNode is null inside the while loop. Here is the corrected version of your code:

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode removeElements(ListNode head, int val) {
        if(head==null) return null;
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next= head;
        ListNode prev = dummy;
        ListNode cur = head;
        while(cur!=null){
            if(cur.val==val){
                prev.next=cur.next;
            }
            else{
                 prev=cur;
            }
            cur=cur.next;
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

In this code, dummy node is introduced as a fake head of the list. This will handle the cases when the given (val) is at the head of the list. In the while loop, I am checking if current node's value is equal to given value (val), if yes then delete that node by linking previous node's next pointer to the next node of current, skipping the current node. If value is not equal, then just move the previous pointer to current.

We also move current to the next node in each iteration. This means we have a pointer to keep track of the node before the node we are checking (prev), and the node we're checking against (cur). If a match is found, we neatly snip it out of the list utilizing this system.

