Here is a Python solution that uses depth-first search to solve this problem. In this recursive DFS approach, we start from the leaves of the tree and work our way up, checking if a subtree is univalue and incrementing a global count if it is.

```python
class Solution:
    def countUnivalSubtrees(self, root):
        if not root: 
            return 0
        self.count = 0
        self.is_uni(root)
        return self.count

    def is_uni(self, node):
        if not node.left and not node.right:
            self.count += 1
            return True
        is_uni = True
        if node.left:
            is_uni = self.is_uni(node.left) and is_uni and node.left.val == node.val
        if node.right:
            is_uni = self.is_uni(node.right) and is_uni and node.right.val == node.val
        self.count += is_uni
        return is_uni
```

The `is_uni` function checks if a subtree is univalue. It first checks if it is at a leaf node, in which case it increments the count and returns True, as a leaf node is inherently a univalue subtree.  It then recursively checks the left and right subtrees of the node to see if they are univalue. If they are and their values are the same as the current node, it increments the count and returns True. If not, it returns False. 

This approach has a runtime complexity of O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the tree, since every node is visited once. This is relatively efficient, especially given the constraint that the total number of nodes will be in the range [0, 1000]. Therefore, this solution should work for most scenarios.

