Accessing a model from inside another is not quite recommended because it somehow violates the essence of MVC architecture. However, you can break this rule, just at the cost of making your code a bit more complex to manage in the long run.

Here's how to load a model from another model:
```php
class Some_model extends CI_Model {

    function __construct() 
    { 
        parent::__construct(); 
        $this->load->model('model_name'); // Load the model
    }

    function some_function() 
    { 
        $this->model_name->some_other_function(); // Call a function in the so-loaded model
    } 
}
```

But there is a better way to handle this in case of authentication, by using CodeIgniter's built-in session class. 

Just set session data after user logs in:
```php
$this->session->set_userdata('logged_in', TRUE);
```
Before any private action checks if it is set:
```php
if ($this->session->userdata('logged_in') == TRUE) 
{ 
    // proceed with your private content
} 
else 
{ 
    // redirect to login form
}
```
This way, you don't have to load your authentication model in every model or controller where you need to perform such authentication checks. You simply check the session data.

