The problem asks to make a MATLAB function to calculate the weighted interquartile range (IQR). MATLAB do not directly provide function to calculate weighted IQR, so we will first write a custom function for this. Weighted IQR is calculated by first sorting the data points, assigning them weights, calculating the weighted cumulative sum, and then finding the 1st quartile(Q1) and 3rd quartile(Q3) points. Then, weighted IQR is Q3 - Q1.

Consider we have a list as [{[1 2 3], 2}, {[3 5 7], 1}], Here, the 1st element is {[1 2 3], 2}, where [1 2 3] is the list of numbers and 2 is the weight. 

The MATLAB code will be as follows:

```MATLAB
function B = weighted_IQR(n, A)
B = zeros(n,1);

for tuple = 1:n
    arr = A{tuple}{1};
    weight = A{tuple}{2};

    % calculate weighted IQR
    sorted = sort(arr);
    wghts = sorted * weight;
    cum_wghts = cumsum(wghts) / sum(wghts); 

    q1 = interp1q(cum_wghts,sorted,0.25); 
    q3 = interp1q(cum_wghts,sorted,0.75); 
    B(tuple) = q3 - q1;
end
end
```

To test this function, let's create a script with the following content:

```MATLAB
clc;
clear;

A = cell(2,1);
A{1} = {[1 2 3], 1};
A{2} = {[7 7 8], 2};

B = weighted_IQR(2, A);
disp(B);
```

This will calculate the weighted IQR for 2 tuples and print the result "B".

This code uses the built-in MATLAB function "interp1q" for linear interpolation, to estimate the quartiles of the weighted distribution. The line "cum_wghts = cumsum(wghts) / sum(wghts);" calculates the cumulative weights and normalizes them so they sum up to 1. This is equivalent to calculating the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the weighted data. Then, "q1" and "q3" are estimated by finding the 25% and 75% points, respectively, of the weighted CDF.

