In order to change the color of text in a C++ console output, you would have to use system-specific functionality, as the language itself does not inherently support it. 

For Windows, you'd use the `SetConsoleTextAttribute` function; on Unix-based systems, you would use ANSI escape codes. 

Here is an example in which we create separate functions for setting text color on Windows and Unix-based systems, and then a third function which calls one of the first two depending on the operating system:

```C++
#include<cstdlib>

#ifdef _WIN32
    #include<windows.h>
    void SetTextColor(int color){
        HANDLE  hConsole;
        hConsole = GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, color);
    }
#elif defined(__unix__) || defined(__APPLE__)
void SetTextColor(int color){
    std::string command = "setterm -foreground " + std::to_string(color);
    system(command.c_str());
}
#else
    void SetTextColor(int color){}; // Non-supported OS, leave function blank
#endif  

void SetColor(int color){
    #ifdef _WIN32
        SetTextColor(color);
    #elif defined(__unix__) || defined(__APPLE__)
        SetTextColor(color);
    #else
        // OS not supported
        // Can put default operations here
    #endif
}
```
You would then call `SetColor(int color)` function passing the color code as parameter in your main code to change the text color.

