* Create a helper function `is_prime(n)` using trial division up to sqrt(n) to determine primality efficiently.  
* Iterate over all possible divisors `d` starting from 2 up to sqrt(a), since factors larger than sqrt(a) will have corresponding smaller factors already checked.  
* For each divisor `d` that divides `a` exactly, compute the pair of factors (d, a/d).  
* Check if both `d` and the complementary factor `a/d` are prime numbers by calling the helper function `is_prime`.  
* If any such pair of primes is found, immediately return true, as the condition is satisfied.  
* After checking all possible divisors and not finding any valid prime pairs, return false.  
* The algorithm optimizes by only iterating up to the square root of `a`, reducing the number of checks needed significantly.  

```cpp
#include<stdio.h>
using namespace std;
bool is_prime_product(int a) {
    auto is_prime = [&](int n) -> bool {
        if (n <= 1) return false;
        if (n == 2) return true;
        if (n % 2 == 0) return false;
        for (int i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
            if (n % i == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    };
    for (int d = 2; d * d <= a; d++) {
        if (a % d == 0) {
            int other = a / d;
            if (is_prime(d) && is_prime(other)) return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
