Yes, you can handle the `AppDomain.CurrentDomain.AssemblyResolve` event in your application. This event is raised when the resolution of an assembly fails. Inside the event handler, you can specify a different location to look for the assemblies:

```csharp
AppDomain.CurrentDomain.AssemblyResolve += (sender, args) =>
{
    string assemblyName = new AssemblyName(args.Name).Name;
    if (assemblyName.EndsWith(".resources")) return null;

    string path = Path.Combine("path-to-subfolder", assemblyName + ".dll");
    if (!File.Exists(path)) return null;

    return Assembly.LoadFrom(path);
};
```

This code doesn’t consider the situation when the required assembly is a native dll loaded by the PInvoke mechanism. If you have such a case you’ll need to use the Windows API `SetDllDirectory` function because .NET doesn't support this directly.

Note that this event handler will run each time .NET fails to find an assembly, so you should be aware of possible performance implications. Ideally, the handler should be able to quickly determine if a given assembly is one of those that should be loaded from the subfolder, and return as quickly as possible if it isn't.

Also note that even in this situation, verifying that the assembly files actually exist on disk and are accessible is crucial. Failing to do so, or providing incorrect paths to `Assembly.LoadFrom()`, can result in quite puzzling exceptions.

