When you create an array of class objects in C# (or any other object-oriented language), you're not actually creating the objects themselves - you're creating the space to store references to those objects. This is why your "First Try" works fine - you're creating an array of strings, which are objects in C#, and assigning values to them directly.

In your "Second Try", you're creating an array to store references to `player` objects, but you never instantiate the player objects themselves. This is why you're getting a `"Object reference not set to an instance of an object"` error when you try to access the `name` property of `b[0]` and `b[1]` - these array elements are `null`, and you can't access properties or methods on `null` objects.

To fix this, you have to instantiate a `player` object and assign it to the respective index in the array, like so:

```C#
static void Main(string[] args)
{
    #First Try OK
    string[] a = new string[2];
    a[0] = "Jordan";
    a[1] = "Pippen";

    Console.WriteLine(String.Join(",", a));

    #Second Try NG
    player[] b = new player[2];
    b[0] = new player();  // You must instantiate the player object
    b[0].name = "Jordan";
    b[1] = new player();  // You must instantiate the player object
    b[1].name = "Pippen";

    // But, Console.WriteLine() will print the class object type, you need to implement ToString() method to get the name
