This problem requires creating a multi-threaded Java application with capabilities to handle thread contention and deadlock situations. Below is a simplified version of a solution. This code snippet only covers essential parts due to the complexity of the subject, and a full-blown enterprise-grade solution would need much more advanced techniques.

Firstly, the `Thread` class in Java should be extended. When run, this thread will check for contention via a shared `Lock` object. If the lock is available, it will simulate some work then release the lock:

```java
public class MyThread extends Thread {
    private Lock lock;

    public MyThread(String name, Lock lock) {
        super(name);
        this.lock = lock;
    }

    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            try {
                if (lock.tryLock(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                    try {
                        System.out.println(getName() + " is working...");
                        Thread.sleep(1000); // simulate work
                    } finally {
                        lock.unlock();
                    }
                } else {
                    System.out.println("Thread contention detected by " + getName());
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

To run the application, create multiple threads and start:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
        int threadCount = 5;

        for (int i = 0; i < threadCount; i++) {
            new MyThread("Thread" + i, lock).start();
        }
    }
}
```

This application creates five threads that vie for a single lock, thereby creating opportunities for contention. When a thread tries and fails to acquire the lock, it prints a message to alert of the contention.

This is a rudimentary approach for deadlock prevention. For more nuanced scenarios like global order on locks, hierarchy-based, or avoidance by the Banker's algorithm, more advanced code structures would be necessary. Similarly, to capture more comprehensive semantics for handling thread contention, you would need to implement techniques like exponential backoff strategies and advanced synchronized constructs. 

Overall, multithreading, deadlock and resource contention in Java is a vast topic. In fact, entire textbooks and graduate courses address this topic. This simplified response serves as basic insight into what can be a complex topic.

