class Solution {
    constructor(N, blacklist) {
        this.map = new Map();
        this.M = N - blacklist.length;
        const last = new Set(Array.from({length: N-this.M}, (_, i) => i + this.M));
        blacklist.forEach(x => last.delete(x));
        let iter = last.values();
        blacklist.forEach(x => {
           if (x < this.M) { 
               this.map.set(x, iter.next().value);
           }
        });
    }

    pick() {
        let k = Math.floor(Math.random() * this.M);
        return this.map.has(k)? this.map.get(k) : k;
    }
}
```

The main idea is to make a map `map` from black numbers in `[0, M)` to white numbers in `[M, N)` with `M = N−blacklist.length` and randomly pick a number in `[0, M)`. If the number is black, return the corresponding white number; else return the number itself.

In more detail, the gaps between consecutive white numbers in `[M, N)` are filled in by black numbers in `[0, M)`. When initializing, use a `Set` `last` to store all white numbers in `[M, N)`, and remove black numbers from `last`. Then assign the remaining numbers in `last` to black numbers in `[0, M)` one by one.

In `pick`, get a random `k` in `[0, M)`. If `k` is a black number, return `map.get(k)`; else return `k`.

