A Deque is similar to a dynamic array that allows insertion and deletion operations from both the ends.

Here is the JavaScript code for a Deque class using an Array.

```javascript
class Deque {
   constructor() {
      this.items = [];
   }

   // function to check if the deque is empty
   isEmpty() {
      return this.items.length == 0;
   }

   // function to add an element to the front of the deque
   addFront(element) { 
      this.items.unshift(element);
   }

   // function to add an element to the rear of the deque
   addRear(element) {
      this.items.push(element);
   }

   // function to remove an element from the front of the deque
   removeFront() {
      if(this.isEmpty()) {
          return "Underflow";
      }
      return this.items.shift();
   }

   // function to remove an element from the rear of the deque
   removeRear() {
      if(this.isEmpty()) {
           return "Underflow";
      }
      return this.items.pop();
   }

   // function to return the front element of the deque
   getFront() {
      if(this.isEmpty()) {
          return "No elements in Deque";
      }
      return this.items[0];
   }

   // function to return the rear element of the deque
   getRear() {
      if(this.isEmpty()) {
          return "No elements in Deque";
      }
      return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
   }

   // function to display the deque elements
   display() {
      let str = "";
      for(let i = 0; i < this.items.length; i++)
          str += this.items[i] +" ";
      return str;
   }
}
```
The above JavaScript class represents a Deque. The Deque has methods `addFront()` and `addRear()` to add elements at the front and rear respectively. And `removeFront()` and `removeRear()` methods are used to remove the elements from the front and rear respectively. The `getFront()` and `getRear()` methods are used to get the front and rear elements of the deque. The `isEmpty()` method is used to check whether the deque is empty or not, `display()` method to print all the elements in the deque.

