Implementing the complete code for a doubly-linked list optimized for search operations with automatic garbage collection is too complex and lengthy for this platform. However, below is a simplified version of how this can be achieved. 

In this context, it will be most effective to design a C++ class as a template and use a map from the Standard Template Library (STL) instead of hashing for search optimization purpose.

Please note that C++ inherently does not support garbage collection like other languages (Java or Python), but we can manually manage memory to avoid memory leaks. Here is a simplified version of how this can be achieved:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>

template <typename T>
class Node {
public:
    T data;
    Node<T>* prev;
    Node<T>* next;
};

template <typename T>
class DoublyLinkedList {
private:
    Node<T>* head;
    Node<T>* tail;
    std::map<T, Node<T>*> nodeLookup;

public:
    DoublyLinkedList(): head(NULL), tail(NULL) {}

    ~DoublyLinkedList() {
        while(head) {
            Node<T>* next = head->next;
            delete head;
            head = next;
        }
    }

    void insert(T d) {
        Node<T>* node = new Node<T>();
        node->data = d;
        node->prev = NULL;
        node->next = head;
        if(head != NULL) {
           head->prev = node;
        }
        if(tail == NULL) {
           tail = node;
        }
        head = node;
        nodeLookup[d] = node;
    }

    void remove(T d) {
        Node<T>* node = nodeLookup[d];
        if(node == NULL) {
            std::cout << "Node not found" << std::endl;
            return;
        } else {
            if(node->prev != NULL) {
                node->prev->next = node->next;
            } else {
                head = node->next;
            }
            if(node->next != NULL) {
                node->next->prev = node->prev;
            } else {
                tail = node->prev;
            }
            delete node;
            nodeLookup.erase(d);
        }
    }

    void search(T d) {
        Node<T>* node = nodeLookup[d];
        if(node == NULL) {
            std::cout << "Node not found" << std::endl;
        } else {
            std::cout << "Node found: " << node->data << std::endl;
        }
    }
}; 
```
This code provides a doubly linked list of generic type T, and includes a map to track each node. Note that more complex edge cases and error handling, as well as more detailed performance tweaks, are not included in this simplified example. Also, automatic garbage collection is not possible in C++, but you can still take care about the deletion of dynamically allocated nodes in destructor to avoid memory leaks.

