The issue here is that boost::mpi::broadcast and similar functions rely on boost::serialization, but Boost.Geometry doesn't support the boost::serialization serialization method for its rtree index.

You can implement serialization following this example: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22507633/boostrtree-with-custum-serialization 

Once you've defined those, you may be able to use Boost.MPI functions directly.

If Boost.Geometry's rtree index still can't be serialized/deserialized with the native Boost serialization, you can try to bridge the gap using a custom serializer/deserializer like this:

```cpp
boost::geometry::index::rtree< int, boost::geometry::index::quadratic<16> > rtree;
// populate rtree
std::vector<char> buf;  // This will store the serialized rtree
boost::iostreams::back_insert_device<std::vector<char> > inserter(buf);
boost::iostreams::stream<boost::iostreams::back_insert_device<std::vector<char> > > s(inserter);
boost::archive::binary_oarchive oa(s);
oa << rtree;
s.flush();
// Now buf contains the serialized rtree

// Broadcast the size of the buffer and then the buffer itself
int size = buf.size();
boost::mpi::broadcast(comm, size, 0);
buf.resize(size);
boost::mpi::broadcast(comm, &buf.front(), size, 0);

// Now each process deserializes the buffer into its own rtree
boost::iostreams::basic_array_source<char> device(&buf.front(), size);
boost::iostreams::stream<boost::iostreams::basic_array_source<char> > s(device);
boost::archive::binary_iarchive ia(s);
ia >> rtree;
```

This will serialize the rtree into a buffer, broadcast the size of that buffer and then the buffer itself, and then deserialize the buffer into each process' rtree.

Note that the Boost.Geometry team plans to implement Boost.Serialization support but it's not clear when this will happen.

