Here is a Java code to solve this problem. The principle used here is Bit Manipulation. It makes use of the behavior of negative numbers in Java (and most other languages) which are stored as 2's complement.

2's complement representation helps us differentiate between positive and negative numbers. When the MSB(most significant bit) is 1, the number is negative and when it is 0, the number is positive. And for negative numbers, the rest of the bits contain the 2's complement of the absolute value of the number.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int a = 3;
            int b = 4;

            int c = a - b;
            int k = (c >> 31) & 0x1;

            int maximum = a - k * c;

            System.out.println("The maximum of two numbers is " + maximum);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Exception occurred: " + e);
        }
    }
}
```
This code subtracts `b` from `a` and stores the result in `c`. If `a > b`, `c` will be positive, otherwise `c` will be negative.

Then, `(c >> 31) & 0x1` will do a bitwise right shift on `c` by `31`. If `c` was positive, this value will be `0` as shifting a positive integer by 31 places to the right results in `0`. If `c` was negative, this value will be `1`.

Then, `a - k * c` will give us the maximum of `a` and `b`. If `k` is `0`, it means `a > b` and so maximum is `a`. If `k` is `1`, it means `b > a` and `a - c` will result in `b`. Thus maximum is `b`.

