Analyzing the words in a sentence and identifying the parts of speech in a Java program requires natural language processing (NLP) capabilities. It's not something Java provides out-of-the-box. Java-based libraries like Stanford's CoreNLP or OpenNLP can be used to perform such tasks. In the following example, I will use Stanford's CoreNLP library.

Before running the example, be sure to download Stanford CoreNLP library files and add them to your project's build path. 

Here is the Java code:

```java
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.simple.*;

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a string, in this case a sentence.
        String text = "My name is John.";

        // Create the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
        Properties properties = new Properties();
        properties.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize,ssplit,pos");
        StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(properties);

        // Annotate an example text.
        Annotation ann = new Annotation(text);
        pipeline.annotate(ann);

        // Iterate over all the sentences found
        List<CoreMap> sentences = ann.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
        for (CoreMap sentence : sentences) {
            // Iterate over all tokens in a sentence
            for (CoreLabel token: sentence.get(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation.class)) {
                // Retrieve and print the token's POS tag
                String pos = token.get(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
                System.out.println("Token: " + token + ", POS: " + pos);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The output of this program would be:

```
Token: My, POS: PRP$
Token: name, POS: NN
Token: is, POS: VBZ
Token: John, POS: NNP
Token: ., POS: .
```

Parts of speech are abbreviated (PRP$ for possessive pronoun, NN for noun,singular or mass, VBZ for verb, 3rd person sing. present, NNP for proper noun, singular and . for punctuation mark, period).
For more information on Parts Of Speech tags refer here -> https://www.ling.upenn.edu/courses/Fall_2003/ling001/penn_treebank_pos.html

