The command line in question will output the text "Hello, World" to the console, but this is an overly simplified explanation. Let's dissect this further:

In C++, the 'cout' object is an instance of the 'ostream' class, which is part of the 'iostream' library. 'cout' is directly associated with the standard output device, which is usually the terminal or command line.

Here's how it's normally used:

```c++
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello, World";
    return 0;
}
```
The '<<' operator is the stream insertion operator. When used with 'cout', it sends whatever is on its right to the standard output device. In this case, that's the string "Hello, World".

The '\n' (newline) or endl is often used after the string. Without it, the program control will remain on the same line (which could result in messy output if anything else prints to the console). Here's an example with a newline:

```c++
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello, World\n";
    return 0;
}
```
When this program is run, "Hello, World" will be printed to the console and then the console's cursor will be moved to the next line, ready for any further output.

