Is it possible to build StringEnum interface to make sure every enum has find(), toString() and a constructor?

Thanks.

Interfaces in Java can include method signatures, but not constructors. So, the class implementing the interface can have any number of constructors. It's also important to note that an enum can't implement an interface in a way a class does, but it can be used to define methods defined in the interface.

That being said, you can use an interface to ensure both `find()` and `toString()` methods, but you cannot enforce a specific constructor via an interface. Here is how your interface and enum can look like:

```java
public interface StringEnum {

    String toString();

    static <E extends Enum<?> & StringEnum> E find(String value, Class<E> enumClass) {
        for(E enumValue: enumClass.getEnumConstants()) {
            if(enumValue.toString().equals(value)) {
                return enumValue;
            }
        }

        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown value: " + value );
    }
}

public enum Status implements StringEnum { 

    ONLINE("on"),OFFLINE("off");

    private String status = null;

    private Status(String status) {
        this.status = status;
    }

    public String toString() {
        return this.status;
    }
}
```

Here, `find()` method is implemented as a static method inside the interface, and you need to provide the class of the specific enum you are searching in as an argument. Then you can call this method like so: `StringEnum.find("on", Status.class);`. 

This approach works if you have several enums implementing the `StringEnum` interface, and you want a 'find' functionality for all of them.

For the enum itself, note that the `status` variable and the constructor can't be part of the interface.

