Here is how you can solve this problem using Object-Oriented Programming in JavaScript.

First, we create the general Animal object (using the function-based constructor syntax in JavaScript):

```javascript
function Animal(type, name, age, isEndangered) {
  this.type = type;
  this.name = name;
  this.age = age;
  this.isEndangered = isEndangered;
}

Animal.prototype.speak = function() {
  return this.name + " makes a noise.";
}

Animal.prototype.grow = function() {
  this.age += 1;
}
```

We then define the specific animal object (in this case, a Lion) that inherits from the Animal object:

```javascript
function Lion(type, name, age, isEndangered, habitat) {
  Animal.call(this, type, name, age, isEndangered);
  this.habitat = habitat;
}

Lion.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype);
Lion.prototype.constructor = Lion;

Lion.prototype.hunt = function() {
  return this.name + " is hunting.";
}
```

You can then create a new Lion object and use all the methods as per the problem statement:

```javascript
const lion = new Lion("Lion", "Simba", 5, true, "Savannah");

console.log(lion.type); // => "Lion"
console.log(lion.name); // => "Simba"
console.log(lion.age); // => 5
console.log(lion.isEndangered); // => true
console.log(lion.habitat); // => "Savannah"

console.log(lion.speak()); // => "Simba makes a noise."
lion.grow();
console.log(lion.age); // => 6

console.log(lion.hunt()); // => "Simba is hunting."
```

In this program, the `Animal.call(this, type, name, age, isEndangered)` line inside the Lion constructor is used to call the parent constructor with the current context, effectively initializing the inherited properties. The `Object.create(Animal.prototype)` line is used to inherit the methods from the Animal prototype.

def expand_around_center(s, left, right):
    while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
        left -= 1
        right += 1
    return right - left - 1

def main():
    s = input().strip()
    if not s:
        print("")
        return
    start, end = 0, 0
    for i in range(len(s)):
        len1 = expand_around_center(s, i, i)   # Odd length palindromes
        len2 = expand_around_center(s, i, i + 1) # Even length palindromes
        max_len = max(len1, len2)
        if max_len > (end - start):
            start = i - (max_len - 1) // 2
            end = i + max_len // 2
    print(s[start:end + 1])

