```php
<?php
function remove_vowels_digits_punctuation_whitespace($text) {
    /*  The regular expression is saying, in order: 
        1. start the search.
        2. `[a-z] negates small english alphabets from a-z.
        3. `[A-Z] negates capital english alphabets from A-Z.
        4. `\s` negates whitespace characters.
        5. `u` is for UNICODE
        This will remove all characters that are not English letters and not whitespace.
    */
    $text = preg_replace('/[^a-zA-Z\s]/u', '', $text);

    /*  
        `str_replace` will replace the given characters (array elements) in string with empty string.
        Here, it's used to remove punctuation.
    */
    $text = str_replace(str_split('!"#$%&\'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[\]^_`{|}~'), '', $text);

    /*  
        `str_replace` with `array(" ", "\n", "\r", "\t", "\0", "\x0B")` remove all kind of white spaces.
    */
    $text = str_replace(array(" ", "\n", "\r", "\t", "\0", "\x0B"), '', $text);

    /*  
        `str_replace` with arrays of vowels (lowercase and uppercase) remove all the vowels from the string. 
    */
    $text = str_replace(array('a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u', 'A', 'E', 'I', 'O', 'U'), '', $text);

    return $text;
}
?>
```

This function performs the same three operations (removing non-alphabetical characters, punctuation, and whitespace) first, and then removes any vowels from the result. It's reusing `str_replace` to remove the unwanted characters as it methodically works through the string.

