Based on the details you provided, it seems there is a problem regarding the permissions or settings in your WebView. If not addressed properly, the WebView might not display or play the camera stream unless user input is detected (in your case, touching the screen).

Here are some suggestions:

1. Make sure you have correctly set up permissions in your AndroidManifest.xml: 

Before accessing the camera you need to ensure that the required permission is declared. Add these within the `<manifest>` element:

```
<uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="true" />
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
```

Also, starting from Android 6.0 (API level 23), users grant permissions to apps while the app is running, not when they install the app. So, make sure you have implemented runtime permissions correctly.

2. Use WebSettings and enable JavaScript:

You need to ensure that JavaScript is enabled in the WebView, as getUserMedia() function you are using is JavaScript-based. Add these lines after the WebView's initialization:

```java
WebSettings webSettings = webView.getSettings();
webSettings.setJavaScriptEnabled(true);
```

3. Allow WebView to access camera:

WebView needs to have setWebChromeClient and overridden `onPermissionRequest` set to grant permission on WebView. Like this:

```java
webView.setWebChromeClient(new WebChromeClient() {
    @Override
    public void onPermissionRequest(final PermissionRequest request) {
        request.grant(request.getResources());
    }
});
```

