One way to solve this is to implement your own autocloseable logic.

You could wrap your `Jedis` in a custom class and override the `close()` method to return it to the pool instead of closing it. Then in `whenComplete`, you can close the wrapper, which will return the jedis instance to the pool.

Here is an example class:

```java
public class PooledJedis implements AutoCloseable {

    private final JedisPool pool;
    private final Jedis jedis;

    public PooledJedis(JedisPool pool, Jedis jedis) {
        this.pool = pool;
        this.jedis = jedis;
    }

    public Jedis getJedis() {
        return jedis;
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        pool.returnResource(jedis);
    }
}
```

And the where you used:

