Write a C++ function that removes all the duplicates in a given array and returns the new length of the modified array. Additionally, find and document the error(s) in the provided erroneous code snippet.

## Given Erroneous Code Snippet

```cpp
int removeDuplicates(int arr[], int n) {

    if (n == 0 || n == 1)
        return n;

    int temp[n];

    int j = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++)
        if (arr[i] != arr[i+1])
            temp[j++] = arr[i];

    temp[j++] = arr[n-1];
    for (j = 0; j < n; j++)
        arr[j] = temp[j];

    return j;
}
```

## Errors in given code snippet:

1. The code assumes that the array is sorted. The function will not work on an array that isn't sorted because it only checks the next item in the array.
2. The function `removeDuplicates` creates a new array of the same size. The function copies over only distinct elements, so the new array might be smaller. However, it then copies over all the elements into the original array, even the unused parts of the new array. This is an error because it writes invalid data back to the original array.
3. The return value of the function (`j` at the end of the function) is not correct. Here `j` holds the value equal to the size of the initial array `n` and not the size of the array that has no duplicates.

## Corrected Code:
```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

// This function returns new size of the modified array.
int removeDuplicates(int arr[], int n) {

    // Check if the array is empty or contains one element
    if (n == 0 || n == 1)
        return n;

    // Create another array to store unique elements
    vector<int> temp(n);

    // Used as index in the modified array
    int j = 0;

    // Traverse through the array. 
    // If current element is not equal to previous element,
    // store it in temp array
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)
        if (arr[i] != arr[i + 1])
            temp[j++] = arr[i];

    // Store the last element
    temp[j++] = arr[n - 1];

    // Resize temp array
    temp.resize(j);

    // Overwrite the original array with unique elements
    for (int i = 0; i < j; i++)
        arr[i] = temp[i];

    // Return size of the array without duplicates
    return j;
}
```

Please note, you can also use `std::unique` function of C++ STL which can do this work in one line. It removes the consecutive duplicates in range [first, last) and returns a past-the-end iterator for the new logical end of the range. For this, you also need to pass the array with sorted data.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int removeDuplicates(int arr[], int n)  {
    if (n==0 || n==1)
        return n;

    auto end = unique(arr, arr + n);
    return distance(arr, end);
}
```

