[DllImport("coredll.dll", SetLastError = true)]

private static extern int GetModuleFileName(IntPtr hModule, byte[] lpFilename, int nSize);

Yes, you're correct. There is indeed no built-in method to directly retrieve the name of the current executing assembly in Compact Framework. Your solution using GetModuleFileName function from coredll.dll is a common workaround. Here is an expanded version of the solution you posted which is a bit clearer to understand:

```Csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Text;

public class Program
{
    [DllImport("coredll.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    static extern int GetModuleFileName(IntPtr hModule, byte[] lpFilename, int nSize);
    static int MAX_PATH = 260;

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        byte[] buffer = new byte[MAX_PATH * 2];
        int chars = GetModuleFileName(IntPtr.Zero, buffer, MAX_PATH);
        if (chars > 0)
        {
            string assemblyPath = Encoding.Unicode.GetString(buffer, 0, chars * 2);
            Console.WriteLine("Assembly Path: " + assemblyPath);
        }
    }
}
```
In this solution, the native GetModuleFileName method is exposed via PInvoke, and then called by passing `IntPtr.Zero` as the module handle, which causes the function to retrieve the path of the executable file of the current process.

The interpreted byte array is then converted to a string and printed to the console. The assembly name can be extracted from the `assemblyPath`.

