Below is a Java code snippet that uses a depth-first search algorithm to traverse a binary search tree and identifies and returns nodes with a value greater than a specified input. In this code, I've used an inorder traversal (left->root->right), which gives us sorted order for a Binary Search Tree.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Node {
    int value;
    Node left;
    Node right;

    Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        left = null;
        right = null;
    }
}

public class BinaryTree {
    Node root;

    public void inOrderDFS(Node node, int input, List<Integer> result) {
        if (node != null) {
            inOrderDFS(node.left, input, result);

            if (node.value > input) {
                result.add(node.value);
            }

            inOrderDFS(node.right, input, result);
        }
    }

    public List<Integer> greaterValues(int input) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        inOrderDFS(root, input, result);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BinaryTree tree = new BinaryTree();
        tree.root = new Node(10);
        tree.root.left = new Node(5);
        tree.root.right = new Node(20);
        tree.root.left.left = new Node(3);
        tree.root.left.right = new Node(7);
        tree.root.right.left = new Node(15);
        tree.root.right.right = new Node(25);
        List<Integer> result = tree.greaterValues(10);
        for (Integer i : result) {
            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }
}
```

In the main method, a binary search tree is created. The greaterValues method is then called with 10 as the input value. The output of this code will be nodes that have values greater than 10 in ascending order.

Note: You could also use a Stack to implement the DFS, but in Java, the recursive solution is easier and more readable.

