Bubble Sort is considered one of the simplest sorting algorithms. It repeatedly steps through the list, compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The pass through the list is repeated until the list is sorted.

In Javascript, you might implement an optimized version of bubble sort as follows:

```javascript
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let len = arr.length;
  let swapped;
  do {
    swapped = false;
    for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
        //check if the next number is less than current number, then swap
        if (arr[i] < arr[i + 1]) {
          let temp = arr[i];
          arr[i] = arr[i + 1];
          arr[i + 1] = temp;
          swapped = true;
        }
    }
    //optimization: decrease the length of inner loop, since the largest element is already moved to the end after one pass
    len--;
  } while (swapped);
  return arr;
}

console.log(bubbleSort([34,56,4,10,77,51,93,30,5,52]));
```

Running this code will result in the array sorted in descending order.

As for the computational complexity of this algorithm, bubble sort is known to be a heavy and inefficient algorithm when it comes to dealing with a large list of items. The worst-case (and average) time complexity of a bubble sort, even an optimized one, is O(n^2). This is because in both cases each element in the list is compared to every other element.

The space complexity of bubble sort is O(1), because only a single additional memory space is required i.e., for the swapping of elements. This makes it quite a space efficient algorithm. But the low efficiency in time complexity makes it a rarely used algorithm for large datasets.

