            if (n < 0) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input can not be negative");
            }
            long[] fibonacciSeries = new long[n+2];
            fibonacciSeries[0] = 0;  
            fibonacciSeries[1] = 1;  

            System.out.println("Fibonacci series: ");

            for(int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
                if (i > 1) {  
                    fibonacciSeries[i] = fibonacciSeries[i-1] + fibonacciSeries[i-2];  
                }
                System.out.print(fibonacciSeries[i] + " ");
            }
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input, Please input a positive integer");
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e1) {
            System.out.println(e1.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

This program uses dynamic programming to generate the Fibonacci series, which ensures an optimal time complexity of O(n). The `try-catch` blocks are used to handle exceptions that may occur if the input is not a positive integer. The `InputMismatchException` is thrown when the input provided by the user is not an integer, and the `IllegalArgumentException` is thrown when the input is a negative integer.

Please note:
The code will throw an `OutOfMemoryError` if the input number is too large (greater than 2^31-1), since an array of size `n+2` is created. The size of an array in Java can be at most `Integer.MAX_VALUE`.

