```javascript
function smallestNumbers(arr) {
    let smallest = Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER;
    let occurrences = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < smallest) {
            smallest = arr[i];
            occurrences = 1;
        } else if (arr[i] === smallest) {
            occurrences++;
        }
    }

    return Array(occurrences).fill(smallest);
}
```

This function initializes with the largest safe integer in JavaScript as the smallest number. Then it iterates through the array, checking each number. If it finds a smaller number, it sets `smallest` to that new number and resets `occurrences` to 1. If it then finds that number again, it increments `occurrences`.

After it finishes iterating through the array, the function returns an array filled with the smallest number, repeated `occurrences` times. Therefore, if there are multiple smallest integers, it will return all occurrences. This function operates in a time complexity of O(n), where n is the size of the array, because it only requires a single pass through the array.

