The jQuery method you're using assumes a more traditional HTML structure where you can manipulate the DOM elements any time you want.

However in React, things work a bit differently. In React, the rendered HTML elements are representations of a state. Whenever the state changes, React intelligently re-renders the elements. This is the core mechanism that allow React applications to be very performant.

To add a class to an element in React, you need a piece of state to track whether the element should have the class. Then, you use that piece of state to conditionally add the class to the element during rendering.

Here is how it would look in your case:

```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

const [selectedItemId, setSelectedItemId] = useState(null);

const handleRadioButtonClick = (itemId) => setSelectedItemId(itemId);

{
  list.map((item, count) => {
    const isSelected = selectedItemId === item.id.toString();
    return (
        <FormControlLabel
          key={`radio${item.id}${count + 2}`}
          value={item.id.toString()}
          control={
            <Radio 
              color="primary" 
              required={item.required} 
              classes={{checked: isSelected ? 'my-selected-item' : ''}}
            />
          }
          label={getChoice(item)}
          onClick={() => handleRadioButtonClick(item.id.toString())}
        />
    )
  })
}
```
In this code:
- `useState` is used to create a piece of state `selectedItemId` and the function to update it `setSelectedItemId`.
- `handleRadioButtonClick` is the function that updates `selectedItemId` when a radio button is clicked.
- `classes={{checked: isSelected ? 'my-selected-item' : ''}}`: this tells React to add the class `my-selected-item` to the Radio button when it's value is equal to `selectedItemId`.
- `onClick={() => handleRadioButtonClick(item.id.toString())}`: this is the click event handler that will call `handleRadioButtonClick` with `item.id` as an argument when the label is clicked.

