Spring framework provides a nice way to pass command line arguments to your application and use them in your beans.

Instead of parsing the command line arguments manually before bootstrapping your Spring application, you can simply pass them directly to the `SpringApplication.run()` method like this:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    SpringApplication.run(MySpringApplication.class, args);
}
```

Spring Boot will automatically add them as `ApplicationArguments` beans in the ApplicationContext, which you can then inject into your beans like this:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationArguments;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class SomeBean {
    @Autowired
    public SomeBean(ApplicationArguments args) {
        boolean debug = args.containsOption("debug"); // suppose you have used --debug option while running your app

        List<String> files = args.getNonOptionArgs(); // returns ["myfile.txt"] if you run with command line arguments: "myfile.txt"
        // do something with these arguments
    }
}
```

Note that Spring parses command line parameters prefixed with "--" as option arguments (i.e., `args.getOptionNames()`), and parameters without "--" prefix as non-option arguments (i.e., `argsgetNonOptionArgs()`).

This way, you don't have to separate parsing command line arguments and initializing Spring context; Spring framework manages it for you in a much cleaner way.

