In general, it is fairly standard for applications to store per-user settings in a subdirectory of the user's home directory; and this is indeed typically the recommended practice on all three of the operating systems you've listed.

For Windows, the best practice is to store user-specific application data in the directory specified by the AppData environment variable. This corresponds to the user's "Application Data" directory in Windows XP (and similar), and to a similarly-intended directory in Windows Vista and later (typically located within the user's profile directory). This has the advantage that it continues to work correctly even if the user has redirected their "Application Data" directory to a non-standard location.

The actual command could be something as follows to get the AppData directory:

```cpp
char* appdata = getenv("APPDATA");
if (appdata)
{
    printf("APPDATA: %s\n", appdata);
}
```

For MacOS and Linux, it is common practice to create a directory with a name starting with a dot (which makes it a "hidden" directory in Unix parlance), and store any user-specific settings for your application there. The location of this directory would typically be "$HOME/.yourapp". You can get the home directory in both Linux and MacOS with:

```cpp
const char* homeDir = getenv("HOME");
```

This has the advantage that it keeps the user's home directory visually uncluttered when they're looking at it with a GUI file manager or using `ls` on the command line.

It's also worth noting that you should use these locations for storing user-specific data (such as configuration settings), not for shared data or install files.

