Yes, you can differentiate layout based on level of the log message using log4net. To achieve that, you can use a customized PatternLayout and a LevelMatchFilter.

Here is an example in C#.

You have to create a PatternLayout class and override the `Format` method where you will check the Level of the LoggingEvent.

```csharp
public class MyPatternLayout : PatternLayout
{
    private PatternLayout fatalLayout;
    public string FatalPattern { get; set; }

    public override void ActivateOptions()
    {
        base.ActivateOptions();
        fatalLayout = new PatternLayout { ConversionPattern = FatalPattern };
        fatalLayout.ActivateOptions();
    }

    public override void Format(TextWriter writer, log4net.Core.LoggingEvent loggingEvent)
    {
        if (loggingEvent.Level == log4net.Core.Level.Fatal)
        {
            fatalLayout.Format(writer, loggingEvent);
        }
        else
        {
            base.Format(writer, loggingEvent);
        }
    }
}
```

And use it in your log4net configuration:

```xml
<layout type="Namespace.MyPatternLayout, Assembly">
    <conversionPattern value="%date %message%newline" />
    <fatalPattern value="%date [%thread] %-5level %logger - %message%newline" />
</layout>
```

In this example, the `conversionPattern` will be used for all log levels except for Fatal, for which the `fatalPattern` will be used. You can adjust the patterns according to your needs. 

However, keep in mind that getting class name, method name, line number etc., would require the log call to be made with `log4net.LogManager.GetLogger(MethodBase.GetCurrentMethod().DeclaringType)`, as the logger needs to be aware of the method that it's logging from. This can have performance impacts. 

As for the requirement to log all the previous 512 messages into a file when a fatal error occurs, you might need a custom BufferingAppender that only flushes when receiving a message with a fatal level. This is more complex and can increase the memory consumption of your program.
One way to lower the performance impact, if you do not want such detailed information for each log level, is to check the log level before performing the log call. 

For example:
```csharp
if (log.IsFatalEnabled)
{
    log.Fatal("Fatal log message", exception);
}
else if (log.IsDebugEnabled)
{
    log.Debug("Debug log message");
} 
// Etc. for other log levels
```
This way the potentially expensive log call is only done if the log message will actually be emitted.

def factorial(n, memo={}):
    # Handle negative input case
    if n < 0:
        return "Factorial is not defined for negative numbers."
    # Factorial of 0 and 1 is 1
    if n == 0 or n == 1:
        return 1
    # Return memoized result if available
    if n in memo:
        return memo[n]
    # Recursive calculation with memoization
    memo[n] = n * factorial(n - 1, memo)
    return memo[n]

