```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
        self.data = data
        self.next = None

class Queue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.front = self.rear = None

    def isEmpty(self):
        return self.front == None

    def EnQueue(self, item):
        temp = Node(item)
        if self.rear == None:
            self.front = self.rear = temp
            return
        self.rear.next = temp
        self.rear = temp

    def DeQueue(self):
        if self.isEmpty():
            return
        temp = self.front
        self.front = temp.next
        if(self.front == None):
            self.rear = None

    def size(self):
        count = 0
        temp = self.front
        while temp:
            temp = temp.next
            count += 1
        return count
```

This size method helps us to know the number of elements in the queue at any point in time, hence giving us an idea of the queue's capacity before deciding to enqueue or dequeue. This might not elevate performance, but, certainly, it helps in the better management of the queue and enriches its functionality.

