In C#, you can use the `Math.Floor` method to achieve similar results. It returns the largest integer less than or equal to the specified double-precision floating-point number.

```CSharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(ExtractInteger(4.7));  // Output: 4
        Console.WriteLine(ExtractInteger(-3.2));  // Output: -4
    }

    public static int ExtractInteger(double number)
    {
        return (int)Math.Floor(number);
    }
}
```

Here is the explanation of C# version:
C# also includes a class named `Math` which contains different static methods for performing mathematical tasks. The `Floor` method of this class returns the largest integer smaller than or equal to the provided decimal number.

As C# is a statically typed language, it is necessary to explicitly cast the result of the `Math.Floor` function to an `int` using `(int)` before the function call. This is because `Math.Floor` function returns a `double` by default.

Another difference to Python is that in C#, methods are declared within classes. In the provided code, I've created a static method `ExtractInteger` inside a `Program` class. The `Main` method is the entry point of the program where we call our `ExtractInteger` method with sample inputs.

Note: C#'s `Math.Floor` returns the same number if it is already an integer but it will always lean to the left on the number line (towards negative infinity) if it isn't, which is why it returns `-4` when given `-3.2` as input, matching the functionality in the provided python code.

