This problem can be solved by using recursive Depth-First Search (DFS). 

Python code for this is as follows: 

```python
class Solution:
     def maxAncestorDiff(self, root):
            def recursive(node, min_value, max_value):
                if not node:
                    return max_value - min_value
                max_value = max(max_value, node.val)
                min_value = min(min_value, node.val)
                return max(recursive(node.left, min_value, max_value), recursive(node.right, min_value, max_value))
            return recursive(root, root.val, root.val)
```

In this code, we first check if the node exists, if it doesn't, we return the difference between the max_value and min_value that we have found so far. Then, we update max_value and min_value with the value of the current node. Finally, we return the maximum difference from the left and right subtrees.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(N) because each node is visited once and O(1) work is done at each node, hence the total work done is proportional to the size of the tree, i.e., the number of nodes. The space complexity of this algorithm is O(H), where H denotes the height of the tree. In the worst case scenario, a recursive function's call stack could go as deep as the height of the tree, in the case of a skewed binary tree.

This function can handle large binary trees efficiently and fulfills the original constraints of the problem.

