Yes, C Standard Library (libc) has `tmpfile()` and `tmpnam()` functions that can be used to generate unique temporary files. However, `tmpnam()` can be dangerous and is generally considered deprecated.

Instead, it's much safer to use `mkstemp()`.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    char template[] = "/tmp/myprogXXXXXX";
    int fd = mkstemp(template);

    if (fd == -1) {
        /* Handle error */
    }

    /* Now you can write to and read from the file through fd. */

    /* Be sure to unlink/close it when you're done. */
    remove(template);
    close(fd);

    return 0;
}
```

In the example above, the `XXXXXX` is replaced with a unique string by `mkstemp()`. Thus it will create a file with a unique name within /tmp directory.

Remember to handle error scenarios such as the function returning -1.

Also it's crucial to close and remove the tempfile when you're done with it. Be aware that even though the file is removed, the space isn't freed until the file descriptor is closed.

The reason it's secure is because `mkstemp()` atomically creates the file and opens it, assuring that no other process can end up accessing the same file. It returns a file descriptor that you can use to read and write to the file. 

Be sure to read the man page `man 3 mkstemp` to understand the nuances and return values of the function before using it.

