In both Java and JavaScript SDKs for Cloud Spanner, there seems no option to turn off deserialization. This is because these libraries are built upon the protobuf wire format that Google Spanner uses internally to transfer data, and they provide an abstraction to users for comfortably parsing the protobuf packets into language-specific data types.

To mitigate this issue, you might consider following these strategies:

1. Stream the results: Instead of pulling large sets of data into memory, gradually stream them to the next application. This would help in managing the resources better. Cloud Spanner SDKs have APIs that provide streaming option, you might consider exploring those.

Java SDK:

```
 try (Spanner spanner = SpannerOptions.newBuilder().build().getService()) {
   DatabaseId db = DatabaseId.of("my-project", "my-instance", "my-database");
   DatabaseClient client = spanner.getDatabaseClient(db);
   ResultSet resultSet = client.singleUse().executeQuery(Statement.of("SELECT * FROM Users"));
   while (resultSet.next()) {
     // stream the resultSet to your next application here
   }
 }
```

JavaScript SDK:

```js
 var spanner = require('@google-cloud/spanner')({
   projectId: 'my-project',
 });

 var instance = spanner.instance('my-instance');
 var database = instance.database('my-database');

 database.run('SELECT * FROM Users')
   .then(results => {
     const rows = results[0];
     rows.forEach(row => {
       // stream the row to your next application here
     });
   });
```

2. Use raw SQL using JDBC: If you are ok to step away from the SDKs, you can directly interact with Spanner using the JDBC driver and raw SQL commands. You would have more control over what to do with the result set without a need for mandatory Object conversion. This option would need significant code changes though. 

Regardless, ensure the passing of data to the next application is efficient and does not become the next bottleneck.

Also, note that the underlying gRPC protocol (used by SDKs to communicate with Spanner) is highly optimized, capable of exchanging large amounts of data very efficiently. Tweaking the deserialization process may not yield noticeable performance gains and it's usually advisable to focus on optimizing database schema, queries, indexes, and overall system architecture for performance improvements.

