```java
public class PrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 2; i <= 1000; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                System.out.println(i);
            }
        }
    }

    private static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

This java program has a main class called 'PrimeNumbers'. In the main method, it iterates numbers from 2 to 1000. For each number, it checks whether it's a prime number. If it is, the number is printed out. 

An additional static method 'isPrime' is associated with the class to check if a number is prime. If the number is less than or equal to 1, it would not be considered as a prime number, therefore false is returned in this case. If the number is greater than 1, we use a for loop to iterate from 2 to the square root of that number (inclusive). If the number can be divided evenly by any i (i.e., the remainder after division is 0), it's not a prime number, so we return false. If no such i is found after the loop, the number is a prime number and we return true. 

The reason we only need to iterate up to the square root of num is because a larger factor of num would have already been detected while checking smaller multiples, thus improving the time complexity of the algorithm.

