HTML and CSS are commonly used together to structure and style the pages of a website. When dealing with HTML elements that have multiple class attributes, you can select and style these elements using CSS by referencing the class attribute. In CSS, the class selector is designated by a period "." followed by the class name.

For example, an HTML element might be defined as follows:

```html
<div class="class1 class2 class3">Some content</div>
```

This HTML element can be selected in CSS by referencing one of its classes:

```css
.class1 {
  color: blue;
}
```

The div element would then be styled with blue text color. If the class attributes are dynamically changing - such as being added, removed, or modified via JavaScript - you can still select and style them the same way. The styles will simply apply to the elements that have the classes at the time the CSS is applied.

The challenge in working with dynamically changing classes is ensuring that the styles are applied as expected. If a JavaScript function removes a class from an element, the styles that were applied to that class will no longer affect the element. On the other hand, if a class is added to an element, the styles for that class will immediately take effect.

Here is a sample JavaScript code that adds or removes classes from an HTML element dynamically:

```javascript
// Select the first div element
var div = document.querySelector('div');

// Add the 'class4' class
div.classList.add('class4');

// Remove the 'class1' class
div.classList.remove('class1');

// Toggle the 'class2' class (i.e., remove it if it's there, add it if it's not)
div.classList.toggle('class2');
```

The `div` variable is first assigned a reference to the first `div` element in the page. The call to `classList.add('class4')` then adds the 'class4' class to the `div`, and `classList.remove('class1')` removes the 'class1' class. The call to `classList.toggle('class2')` will either add or remove 'class2', depending on whether or not it's currently part of the `div`'s class list.

In summary, dealing with dynamically changing classes requires knowledge of how CSS styles are applied, as well as understanding how to use JavaScript to add, remove, and toggle classes on HTML elements. If done correctly, it can allow you to create highly interactive and responsive web pages.

