Here is a simple Java program that fulfills these criteria. This program uses optimized trial division to check if a number is prime and find its smallest prime factor. The trial division is optimized by dividing only by 2 and the odd integers, as even numbers greater than 2 cannot be prime.

```java
public class MainClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 17;
        int smallestPrimeFactor = findSmallestPrimeFactor(n);

        if (smallestPrimeFactor == n) {
            System.out.println(n + " is a prime number");
        } else {
            System.out.println(n + " is not a prime number. The smallest prime factor is " + smallestPrimeFactor);
        }
    }

    public static int findSmallestPrimeFactor(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("number must be greater than 1");
        }

        // Check division by 2
        if (n % 2 == 0) {
            return 2;
        }

        // Check division by odd numbers up to sqrt(n)
        for (int i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return i;
            }
        }

        // n is prime
        return n;
    }
}
```

In the program, we divide only up to the square root of n because a larger factor of n must be a multiple of a smaller factor that has already been checked.

The time complexity for this algorithm is O( sqrt(n) ) in the worst case.

It works like this: if n is prime, it returns n itself. If n is not prime, it returns the smallest prime factor of n. Before checking for other odd factors, it checks if n is divisible by 2. This same principle applies to larger primes.

