Yes, the common method of enabling multisampling in an OpenGL program is with the help of Window System provided APIs such as WGL (for Windows), GLX (for Unix/Linux), CGL (for Mac OS X), and EGL (for mobile/embedded systems).

However, if you're using GLUT (or something equivalent like freeGLUT or GLFW), it makes creating multi-sampled OpenGL context straightforward and it's multi-platform portable.

Here's an example of enabling multisampling in GLUT:

```cpp
int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    glutInit(&argc, argv);

    // Enable multisampling
    glutInitDisplayMode(GLUT_RGB | GLUT_DOUBLE | GLUT_DEPTH | GLUT_MULTISAMPLE);

    glutInitWindowSize(800, 600);
    glutCreateWindow("Multisampling");

    // Check if multisampling is supported
    if (!glewIsSupported("GL_ARB_multisample"))
    {
        fprintf(stderr, "GL_ARB_multisample not supported!\n");
        return 0;
    }

    // ...
}
```

After creating a multi-sampled OpenGL context, you can use glRenderbufferStorageMultisample and glBlitFramebuffer to enable full scene anti-aliasing:

```cpp
GLuint multiSampleFBO, multiSampleRBO;
glGenFramebuffers(1, &multiSampleFBO);
glGenRenderbuffers(1, &multiSampleRBO);

glBindFramebuffer(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, multiSampleFBO);
glBindRenderbuffer(GL_RENDERBUFFER, multiSampleRBO);
glRenderbufferStorageMultisample(GL_RENDERBUFFER, 4 /*samples*/, GL_RGBA8, width, height);
glFramebufferRenderbuffer(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, GL_COLOR_ATTACHMENT0, GL_RENDERBUFFER, multiSampleRBO);

// Then render your scene...

// Blit multi-sampled buffer to default frame buffer, automatically resolving
// for current read and draw framebuffer :
glBindFramebuffer(GL_DRAW_FRAMEBUFFER, 0);
glBlitFramebuffer(0, 0, width, height, 0, 0, width, height, GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT, GL_NEAREST);
```

Before using the above method, make sure your GPU supports ARB_framebuffer_object extension. Keep in mind that this code is just a snippet, you need to check framebuffer completeness and handle other stuff (like depth buffer).

For non multi-sampling context, you can use glHint(GL_POLYGON_SMOOTH_HINT, GL_NICEST); along with glEnable(GL_POLYGON_SMOOTH); to enable anti-aliasing. However, the quality of anti-aliasing depends on the OpenGL driver's implementation and is usually not as good as multisampling.

