The shared memory is not synchronized between threads in a block. But I don't know if the shared memory is synchronized with the writer thread.
For example, in this example:

__global__ void kernel()
{
    __shared__ int i, j;

    if(threadIdx.x == 0)
    {
        i = 10;
        j = i;
    }

   // #1
}


Is it guaranteed at `#1` that, for thread 0, `i=10` and `j=10`, or do I need some memory fence or introduce a local variable?

In CUDA, threads in a block do not automatically synchronize at each line of code. Therefore, it is possible that, if no synchronization primitive is used, other threads read `i` and `j` before Thread 0 has finished writing.

The synchronization primitive to prevent this is `__syncthreads()`. 

It would look like this:

__global__ void kernel()
{
    __shared__ int i, j;

    if(threadIdx.x == 0)
    {
        i = 10;
        __syncthreads();
        j = i;
        __syncthreads();
    }
}
Now, you are guaranteed that `__syncthreads()` makes sure all threads have reached this point and all memory accesses before this point are visible to all threads. 

Since the writes and reads are in the same thread (Thread 0), without contention from any other threads, the writes will be visible to the subsequent reads in the same thread without needing `__syncthreads()`. 

Therefore, if your question is specifically about Thread 0, then yes, it is guaranteed that `i` will be 10 and `j` will be 10 at `#1` point.

Take note that it's always important to make sure memory is synchronized for all threads, not just Zero, in order to avoid data races.

