To use a custom model binder, follow the steps below:

1. Create Your Custom Binder Class
First, you need to create a custom binder class that implements the IModelBinder interface. You need to implement the BindModel method. Here's an example of how you might create a binder for a Contact:

```csharp
public class ContactBinder : IModelBinder
{
    public object BindModel(ControllerContext controllerContext, ModelBindingContext bindingContext)
    {
        HttpRequestBase request = controllerContext.HttpContext.Request;

        return new Contact
        {
            ID = Convert.ToInt32(request.Form.Get("ID")),
            Name = request.Form.Get("Name"),
            // assign all properties similarly
        };
    }
}
```

2. Register Your Custom Binder
Before you can use your custom binder, you will need to register it in Global.asax, in the Application_Start method:

```csharp
protected void Application_Start()
{
    ModelBinders.Binders.Add(typeof(Contact), new ContactBinder());
    // other configurations..
}
```

3. Use Your Custom Binder In Action Method
Now you're ready to use your custom binder in an action method. Just add your model as an argument and it will be automatically filled in according to your custom binder:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public ActionResult Insert([ModelBinder(typeof(ContactBinder))] Contact contact)
{
    if (this.ViewData.ModelState.IsValid)
    {
        this.contactService.SaveContact(contact);

        return this.RedirectToAction("Details", new { id = contact.ID });
    }

    return View(contact);
}
```

The Contact parameter will be automatically bound by your custom binder, and it will have the values filled in according to your BindModel() method. Note that you only need to specify the custom binder using the [ModelBinder()] attribute if you need to override the binder for a specific action, usually the binder defined in the global.asax is used.

Here is a C# function solution to the problem. The function is named `AreAllCharactersUnique`, and it receives two parameters: `str` which is the string to check, and `caseSensitive` which is a boolean specifying whether the check should be case sensitive or not.

```C#
public static bool AreAllCharactersUnique(string str, bool caseSensitive)
{
    if (!caseSensitive)
        str = str.ToLower();

    return str.Distinct().Count() == str.Length;
}
```

This function works by first converting `str` to all lowercase if `caseSensitive` is false. Then, it uses the `Distinct()` method from LINQ to get a version of `str` where all the characters are unique. If the length of this distinct character string is equal to the length of the original string, that mean all characters are unique.

You can use this function in this way:

```C#
    string str = "Hello";
    bool caseSensitive = false;

    bool result = AreAllCharactersUnique(str, caseSensitive);

    Console.WriteLine(result);  // Prints: False
```

The above code will return false because the string contains the character "l" more than once. If we set `caseSensitive` to true, the function will return true because it will consider lowercase and uppercase characters to be different.

Note: This function uses LINQ (Language Integrated Query), a powerful feature in C# that provides the ability to query objects. It is important to include the directive `using System.Linq;` in your C# file to use LINQ methods.

