A cleaner approach to convert a `stdclass` object to a domain entity in Laravel is by using a factory method inside your entity. This method would transform the `stdclass` object into the required entity. Here is an example:

```php
class UserEntity
{
    protected $name;
    protected $email;

    public function __construct(string $name, string $email)
    {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->email = $email;
    }

    // Factory method to construct a UserEntity from stdclass
    public static function fromStdClass(stdClass $obj): UserEntity
    {
        return new self($obj->name, $obj->email);
    }
}
```

Then, in your repository, you could use this factory method to create entities from DB results :

```php
class UserRepository
{
    public function find($id)
    {
        $userObj = DB::table('users')->where('id', $id)->first();
        return UserEntity::fromStdClass($userObj);
    }
}
```

In the example above with `UserEntity`, the `fromStdClass` method is a factory method that converts a `stdclass` object into an Entity (`UserEntity`). Note the use of `self` to refer to the Entity (`UserEntity`) within the `fromStdClass` method.

This factory method effectively separates the creation of a `UserEntity` from a standard databased class, allowing you to cleanly convert your database retrieval into domain entities.

You can also provide additional checking inside your factory method to ensure the stdclass object contains all the required properties.

