```javascript
const intersection = (arr1, arr2) => arr1.reduce((acc, v) => (arr2.includes(v) && !acc.includes(v) ? [...acc, v] : acc), []);
```

However, this one-liner doesn't meet the third criteria: it won't handle efficiently large input arrays, because includes() method in reduce callback is O(n). So for every element of arr1, we are searching it in array arr2, which leads to O(n^2) time complexity overall. 

A more efficient approach would be to use a HashMap (or an object in JavaScript), which can store elements of the first array and then simply check for each element in the second array whether it's in the HashMap or not. This results in O(n) time complexity. But it can't be achieved in one-liner. Here is the efficient code,

```javascript
function intersection(arr1, arr2) {

  let map = {}, result = [];

  for (let el of arr1) {
    map[el] = true;
  }

  for (let el of arr2) {
    if(map[el] && !result.includes(el)) {
      result.push(el);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

