To specify the `java.naming.factory.initial` and `java.naming.provider.url` as Spring Boot properties, you can do the following:

1. Add properties to your application.properties or application.yml:

```yaml
app.naming.factory.initial = com.sun.jndi.ldap.LdapCtxFactory
app.naming.provider.url = ldap://localhost:389/dc=springframework,dc=org
```

2. Implement an `InitialContextFactory` Bean:

```java
@Configuration
public class JndiConfig {

    @Value("${app.naming.factory.initial}")
    private String initialFactory;

    @Value("${app.naming.provider.url}")
    private String providerUrl;

    @Bean
    public InitialContextFactory jndiFactory() throws NamingException {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty(Context.INITIAL_CONTEXT_FACTORY, initialFactory);
        props.setProperty(Context.PROVIDER_URL, providerUrl);
        return new InitialContext(props);
    }
}
```

IMPORTANT: Your experience may vary depending on the specific `java.naming.factory.initial` you are targeting - as you may need additional properties (e.g. security credentials) when connecting to the server. Some JNDI implementations may not accept properties passed on the fly like this and may still require system properties. 

This proposed solution is a more "Spring" way of configuring and initializing resources, but it's not guaranteed to work for all JNDI providers the same, so you may still be required to stick with system properties.

